#{[{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134698} :activity/id #uuid "dac16d4a-58b8-5e9c-b569-e42a4c036b80" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134698 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135060 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the following inconsistent system of equations:\n\n![](images/6b/6bcdc34077d88eac5ed89d408a1d2525b617a4f427b13433757df918585d380f.svg)\n\nSince the system is inconsistent, we cannot find an exact solution. However, as we've previously seen, we can attempt to find the so-called least-squares solution, which is a vector ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\|  A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b} \\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible. \n\nAs before, the least-squares solution can be found by solving the corresponding normal equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, note the following: \n\n- Previously, we solved the normal equation by left-multiplying the normal equation by $(A^T A)^{-1}.$\n\n- However, in this case, the second column of $A$ is a multiple of the first.\n\n- So, the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent.\n\n- Therefore, $A^T A$ is not invertible.\n\nTo solve the normal equation in this case, we proceed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -20 \\\\ -20 & 80\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the last equation by Gaussian elimination, we obtain the general solution\n\n![](images/b9/b93bd5cb988f208eb26115bfaa08e81be989ee79cf3655612c1a0522a5566d95.svg)\n\nNotice that our solution is not unique! The second term of the least-squares solution (highlighted in blue) represents vectors from the null space of $A.$ \n\nIn general, if a matrix $A$ has collinear columns, then the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf x = \\mathbf b$ is not unique." :tutorial/id #uuid "2f2e227a-264e-5e00-8c12-ea534f4b351f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3637 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "73e4890e-8287-5a80-8c3c-2c95ce05512e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134733}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134699 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9642ac63-6925-5ea3-925d-e697a6222064"} :step/id #uuid "2664b077-4f38-5154-af4c-d4fd21596b27" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134700}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134700 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133802 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bc1e31e-5794-5ae3-8620-6bcaadde6a15"} :step/id #uuid "f4594719-c6f8-53f5-bc1a-22e17b863c14"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134733 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133822 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fda08f5d-4513-5be0-b8c4-475e82f9bf5a"} :step/id #uuid "a43860da-1a59-5a1c-930e-23d6f893c13a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134699}}] :activity/title "The Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System (With Collinearity)" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134027} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415147} :activity/id #uuid "16c4a516-4c29-5524-be1a-6253d16090ae" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415144 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415139 :question/id #uuid "01d4a87d-4b65-5a4c-9736-928bedaa6703"} :step/id #uuid "40c07ba2-e36f-5f6c-9e19-b8d5be6314d0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415145}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415145 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415141 :question/id #uuid "4a92698e-17e4-57b8-8191-7aa9adc0e63f"} :step/id #uuid "b7f52acc-285e-5a90-8147-1ce492061ed5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415148}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415146 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415143 :question/id #uuid "d4a26f8e-8621-57f0-b0fb-289f901931d