m (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-(-1)(2) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda +2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable, and a Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{1}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386411 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3378f655-521b-5110-b38f-1c133f4dd2cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386413} {:answer/id #uuid "e0a7c8ff-eb4b-521b-80b3-4060157145cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386414} {:answer/id #uuid "55f32656-9b33-51fe-8460-916dd3466728" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386415} {:answer/id #uuid "d26c3aec-0f21-5515-9dab-90c4f8258954" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386416} {:answer/id #uuid "bc3eb7ab-8686-51b4-9cb0-d1d100b45e00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386417} :answer-field/id #uuid "7da7fa39-334f-55ce-83cd-598484a339c6" :answer-field/key "selection" 