ligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )-3\\cdot (-3) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=-1.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n![](images/c8/c8716d15efb054f488535b139e201dda7bc61a3fb8880caf50d65bee515c2cc1.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b629bead-e64a-5e73-ae42-80d4ebb3762b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176224 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e1f42a9-2410-5d00-a50f-5c6a17763473" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416994} {:answer/id #uuid "31f893f9-5f3d-577a-921d-33855e0c2e6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416995} {:answer/id #uuid "4d46be50-d6db-513b-9ef7-cbc8edd3addb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416997} {:answer/id #uuid "bc499839-44bb-51b3-bb95-1413004e1a12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417009} {:answer/id #uuid "ce04ef35-1259-5b77-8dbd-404063317f6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417010}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416995} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b4a2d2b-0aea-53f7-a0ec-6d74774ae6df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417014}] :question/id #uuid "8231d418-c039-5ff1-a299-022f1329d9d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-108613" :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix. \n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}4-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-1\\cdot (-1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(3)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176230 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3febb2be-1e0e-57f4-b6ed-5294431dbf2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241754} {:answer/id #uuid "6a31fea1-8bb6-594b-a263-ac4249c16046" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242134} {:answer/id #uuid "c04dbc75-5a51-5999-b545-da162bad5ab0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242863} {:answer/id #uuid "ce8e4b9c-6a6f-5ce3-bc4a-d8fa4a8737b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242997} {:answer/id #uuid "dc417db0-b01e-5493-a115-97232876670e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241754} :answer-field/id #uuid "023d7f78-9f38-5655-93fe-685d7af1bae5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243438}] :question/id #uuid "8fce4ae0-ab7d-5b76-858c-c6d19c621a6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-109094" :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-8-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-8-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-5\\cdot (-5) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(-3)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244229 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "006893c9-19ba-585c-9e32-bfd4faf22536" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241188} {:answer/id #uuid "083a0005-e055-5ef1-ae3f-b4184a601b8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241249} {:answer/id #uuid "3ce00bee-a8cc-5e5e-a94e-eb4ec6402982" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241722} {:answer/id #uuid "ca9920ca-f6cf-5e76-8bf4-40033f6ae117" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242966} {:answer/id #uuid "edbf5faa-5d8f-55bc-8aac-4740cd883d9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241249} :answer-field/id #uuid "5367f0f2-957f-5f2a-9f1c-1decd00abc65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243600}] :question/id #uuid "c401ac87-eb9d-550f-aa95-5fff16627f53" :question/math-academy-id "q-109102" :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )-1\\cdot (-4) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(1)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244271 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1816cc3a-098a-51d7-8a9b-44051edcef01" :answer/type {: