\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-6-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-6-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda )-(-1)\\cdot (4) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda +4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-4.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(-4)=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247275 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c28e38d-7d43-572e-8dc8-5806d62baaac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244562} {:answer/id #uuid "275ac931-c96a-52b5-8682-783dafe0f416" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244659} {:answer/id #uuid "548831ab-b257-5bee-b421-94ce89aa4000" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245057} {:answer/id #uuid "caa17224-d62e-5023-b707-37ece540c3e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246114} {:answer/id #uuid "d38db0a4-5c87-584e-b1e2-36d827004b02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246114} :answer-field/id #uuid "7038d9