/math-academy-id "q-112478" :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix. \n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}10-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 1 & 8-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (10-\\lambda )(8-\\lambda )-(-1)\\cdot 1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-18\\lambda +81 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -9)(\\lambda -9) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=9.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(9)=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417020 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00cd8275-45be-5ddc-b8ef-e32e340faed8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416989} {:answer/id #uuid "116ccc33-25a8-5801-9ef8-97c8196420e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416991} {:answer/id #uuid "345fef9e-2d68-5276-b5a8-66f4a893391e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416996} {:answer/id #uuid "87d699c9-48e9-5acb-be99-b2dbb7b37645" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417004} {:answer/id #uuid "f4cf0c9f-9400-57d8-bda0-74f00f45a5f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}"