"Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 2x2 Scalar Matrix"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3629 :topic/title "Jordan Canonical Form of a 2x2 Matrix"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125443} :activity/id #uuid "e3965249-6bb4-5bb3-b83d-3689a8e9f9e5" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125443 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125800 :tutorial/content "We can use repeated matrix multiplication to determine the state of a system given its initial state and some information about how one state transitions to the next state.\n\nTo explain this concretely, suppose we have a population where people live either in the city or the countryside. A study carried out by the local authority shows that, each year,\n\n- $6\\%$ of the city population relocates to the countryside,\n\n- $3\\%$ of the countryside population relocates to the city, and\n\n- everyone else remains where they are.\n\nThis information can be represented using a diagram, like the one below.\n\n![](images/ab/ab6b3cecac2af22b8665078db56c7daf492c0381434c9c88fe21f2630f743466.png)\n\nWe can also present this information as a table:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"2\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>City</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Countryside</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">To:</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>City</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.94$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.03$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Countryside</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.06$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.97$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nWe want to use this information to determine how the population will be distributed $n$ years after the study was carried out. \n\nFirst, let's turn this table into a matrix $P\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/aa/aa1be872e6f5cdcd842a2f02074f1b9ddd7619697973a2cd06a05f9c1cf9ab75.svg) \n\nNotice that this matrix has the following properties:\n\n- it is a square matrix\n\n- the sum of the entries in each column equals $1$\n\n- all entries in the matrix are non-negative\n\nA square matrix with these properties is called a **stochastic matrix**.\n\nNow suppose that, at the time of the study, $70\\%$ of the population lived in the city and $30\\%$ in the countryside. We can represent this **initial state** using a so-called **stochastic vector** $\\mathbf x_0,$ given by\n\n![](images/35/35a20015fc0e96a78ebf1ea2b8fa49721343279a39a35206d41c5ade0ac78ba8.svg)\n\nStochastic vectors contain non-negative entries only, and the sum of the entries equals $1.$ Note that each column of $P$ is a stochastic vector.\n\nNow that we have our stochastic matrix and initial state vector, we can use matrix multiplication to calculate the population distribution sometime after the initial study took place:\n\n- To calculate the population distribution after one year, we calculate the product $P\\mathbf x_0\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/93/93e30410bcea9b657efe84dc9c32e5f8b3fd3cff159c41f0196547ac5119c766.svg)\nThe resulting vector \n![](images/4b/4b331a70fadd7c061d20b85bb6a7c144de91880f386691f498b1b417f65fa264.svg)\ntells us that one year after the study, $66.7$% of the population will live in the city, and $33.3$% will live in the countryside.\n\n- To model the distribution after $2$ years, we compute $P\\mathbf x_1\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/53/53c774ea2bf14c3f98927d468db9d872c90d11bb8a7ce56f70f611e55faffab8.svg)\nThe resulting vector \n![](images/36/363f748dfe8d79023473d0e3a83f331ec6a92a905ddcc9115b675362a6194f1b.svg)\ntells us that two years after the study, $63.7$% of the population will live in the city, and $36.3$% will live in the countryside." :tutorial/id #uuid "0d87aed7-76da-5126-8eee-f38ced9a46f9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3645 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4f00b242-2400-5948-9531-618af1db558d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125564}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125444 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124544 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abbdd778-7ace-5539-9d6f-cdf01804b237"} :step/id #uuid "2ccc7d00-22a8-5d1a-a0d0-a3e6b10db6f5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125445}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125445 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125801 :tutorial/content "Let's reconsider the population we looked at earlier, where the stochastic vector that represents the initial distribution of people between the city and the countryside is\n\n![](images/a4/a423e2c13f55ff6af245ae6e51c7dfc6618d350f0e6de7ec813db176892be05c.svg)\nand the yearly change in this distribution is represented by the stochastic matrix \n\n![](images/71/714196a216ec63873059d772ce8c548ea644825f62ea32a6047469eaf285a875.svg)\n\nEarlier, we found that the population distribution after $1$ and $2$ years is given by the vectors $\\mathbf x_1$ and $\\mathbf x_2,$ \n respectively, where\n\n![](images/d0/d0b99c9251cef4d82dd48b71d2f4bfc1884f43439a516bd007a1b8ea903c65a5.svg)\n\nand\n\n![](images/48/48982abc258980890c8afd48b2949401e33e2401f8c692e5b1c65b3c4c68705d.svg)\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{x}_0, \\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2, \\ldots$ are called **state vectors**, and $\\mathbf x_0$ is the **initial state**. \n\nTo find the state vector corresponding to the $(k+1)$th year, we left-multiply the state vector corresponding to the $k$th year by $P.$ This gives rise to the following sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1}, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =P{x}_{2}, \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮ \\\\ {x}_{k+1} & =P{x}_{k} \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA sequence of stochastic vectors $\\mathbf{x}_0, \\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2, \\ldots$ related to a stochastic matrix $P$ in this way is called a **Markov chain**." :tutorial/id #uuid "f6720cd1-1b8b-5b03-9092-c29474fc606d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3646 :tutorial/title "Markov Chains"} :step/id #uuid "730b29bf-923b-5d88-9f79-57bed7ca1ca8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125457}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125446 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124545 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e93dde4-a8e8-548a-801e-3d9a320a4513"} :step/id #uuid "bf64f3e5-2318-517c-9967-386760b0f779"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125457 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124554 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4821de45-1b6b-5b4f-be0e-96cf5395bc8d"} :step/id #uuid "c314ef44-f421-5bc5-b410-ab965ccc6457" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125446}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125564 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124614 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66b24a98-c641-5e08-8bec-30f221d1bb22"} :step/id #uuid "d7e8a13c-5f02-5712-9c41-daa1892a5092" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125444}}] :activity/title "Markov Chains" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124711}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186049585 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124544 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124353 :question/id #uuid "e8f30d47-bfd2-50ea-b2ad-d474e2a58d29" :question/math-academy-id "e-5598" :question/problem "There are two groups of people in a particular city: those who use their own vehicles and those who use public transport. A study has shown that, each year\n\n- $35\\%$ of people who used their own vehicles swap to public transport, and\n\n- $25\\%$ of people who used public transport swap to their own vehicles.\n\nGive the stochastic matrix that describes how people change their means of transportation each year." :question/worked-solution "We are given the following situation for each year: \n\n- $35\\%$ of people who used their own vehicles swap to public transport. This implies that $65\\%$ of them remain using their own vehicles.\n\n- $25\\%$ of people who used public transport swap to their own vehicles. This implies that $75\\%$ of them remain using public transport.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"2\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Vehicles</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Public Transport </b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">To:</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Vehicles</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.65$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.25$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Public Transport</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.35$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.75$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nNote that we can write a corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n![](images/f9/f91ceb948ae8dadf7a0ce4750d28e6550a996ce7adeb13e63d6622d5a273625b.svg)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abbdd778-7ace-5539-9d6f-cdf01804b237" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124912 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dd8ae1f-e8a8-5241-9045-e9272d2fa0a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  |  | **Trains** | **Cars** |\n| To: | **Trains** | $0.58$ | $0.33$ |\n| **Cars** | $0.42$ | $0.67$ |  |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122073} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f685fb-39a2-5e2f-bbdc-5f9b124ccc95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  |  | **Trains** | **Cars** |\n| To: | **Trains** | $0.42$ | $0.67$ |\n| **Cars** | $0.58$ | $0.33$ |  |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122074} {:answer/id #uuid "f29284c7-a0a9-5e31-b16c-ad00e206433c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  |  | **Trains** | **Cars** |\n| To: | **Trains** | $0.67$ | $0.58$ |\n| **Cars** | $0.33$ | $0.42$ |  |" :db/id #edb/re