h} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484960} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ef0c785-702b-5715-b3a0-24d72749f491" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485371}] :question/id #uuid "36bba55d-9122-58f3-aa1e-57a8674f5e02" :question/math-academy-id "q-84245" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{18}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{18}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{18}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{18}}\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{8} \\\\ \\sqrt{8} & 4\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind $|\\text{det}(PQ)|$ given that $P$ is an orthogonal matrix." :question/worked-solution "We use the following facts: \n\n- The determinant of a product is equal to the product of the determinants.\n\n- The determinant of an orthogonal matrix $P$ is equal to $\\pm 1.$\n\nApplying these ideas, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\text{det}(PQ)| & =|\\text{det}(P)\\cdot \\text{det}(Q)| \\\\  & =|(\\pm 1)\\cdot \\text{det}(Q)| \\\\  & =|\\pm 1|\\cdot |\\text{det}(Q)| \\\\  & =|\\text{det}(Q)| \\\\  & =|\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 4\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{8}\\cdot \\sqrt{8}| \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Product of Matrices"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133950 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133505 :question/id #uuid "9d50c0e3-386e-57db-b509-bed0d933e6ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-12949" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the orthogonal matrix ![](images/37/37ca77e520fc9797f39e3fb8d381c359a6c7f6f251d17de6ef9586968d5d0d0b.svg)" :question/worked-solution "The inverse of an orthogonal matrix is equal to its transpose.\n\nTherefore, since $D$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{-1} & ={D}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a22cfc-9a78-57ab-93a4-3c3255c34bf7" :knowledge-po