9a-16f2-568a-9eb3-468648e31f8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394581} {:answer/id #uuid "af7d2d13-88e9-5942-bfee-06053135317a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{5} & -\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & -\\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394582} {:answer/id #uuid "9fef2a47-dca7-55c5-b42c-8c63b74bde83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{5} & \\sqrt{5} \\\\ -\\sqrt{5} & -\\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394583} {:answer/id #uuid "3bb0c8fa-24b2-55c3-808d-7c15f12f3c9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D$ is not invertible" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394580} :answer-field/id #uuid "604c75e5-e95e-5d11-96c6-62905a23ed82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394579}] :question/id #uuid "c5d66e4b-7980-5826-8627-e049ba8fb0ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-84255" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the orthogonal matrix $D=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "The inverse of an orthogonal matrix is equal to its transpose.\n\nTherefore, since $D$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{-1} & ={D}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394585 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36c75a5d-1134-57a1-8694-bb4c6254a39d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394587} {:answer/id #uuid "9bdccbd4-8d90-5215-ba50-f48870fe4cfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394588} {:answer/id #uuid "2bf6401d-c382-547a-9138-faa17f596097" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394589} {:answer/id #uuid "62972ff0-981a-52df-949c-8860c2c6a996" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394590} {:answer/id #uuid "1e6f54d4-d899-5100-8481-8abbd46e461f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394591} :answer-field/id #uuid "e90abf70-889e-5953-a2ff-73ae3888c67c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394586}] :question/id #uuid "5d295ce9-f8cd-5701-932f-c89dd90a82fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-84257" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ in $Ax=b$ given that the matrix $A$ is orthogonal, and \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The inverse of an orthogonal matrix is equal to its transpose.\n\nHence, since $A$ is orthogonal, we have ${A}^{-1}={A}^{T}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ {A}^{-1}Ax & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ x & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ x & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ x & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485512 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01ceb64c-0881-5a66-b48f-18aa35dc7f3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484820} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce8b058-46a3-588d-b171-a5e100cbc0ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484909} {:answer/id #uuid "af25de84-a7f8-57b5-bfd8-36f4ca12b320" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485198} {:answer/id #uuid "be6becf1-5606-59ec-9b96-0f266a1a82d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485226} {:answer/id #uuid "ce0c7e1d-bae0-5c82-81a4-18c4d6be60dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485226} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec35b09d-4f30-50dc-a52b-44c7b5d9523b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485470}] :question/id #uuid "340e44da-12e6-5ccb-a96a-bc97b2d0d915" :question/math-academy-id "q-41706" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ in $Ax=b$ given that the matrix $A$ is orthogonal, and \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The inverse of an orthogonal matrix is equal to its transpose.\n\nHence, since $A$ is orthogonal, we have ${A}^{-1}={A}^{T}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ {A}^{-1}Ax & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ x & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ x & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ x & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inverse of an Orthogonal Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133951 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133506 :question/id #uuid "848f3901-12b3-5a37-bb03-08743e7058c8" :question/math-academy-id "e-12950" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the $n\\times n$ orthogonal matrices $P$ and $Q?$\n\nI. $2Q$ is orthogonal\n\nII. $P^{-1}Q$ is orthogonal\n\nIII. $PQ^T$ is orthogonal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ Q^T = Q^T Q = I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have $Q^{-1} = Q^T.$\n\n- $(AB)^T = B^TA^T$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the product $2Q \\cdot (2Q)^T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2Q\\cdot (2Q)^{T} & =2Q\\cdot 2{Q}^{T} \\\\  & =4Q{Q}^{T} \\\\  & =4I \\\\  & \\ne I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the product $(P^{-1}Q)\\cdot (P^{-1}Q)^T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({P}^{-1}Q)\\cdot ({P}^{-1}Q)^{T} & =({P}^{T}Q)\\cdot ({P}^{T}Q)^{T} \\\\  & ={P}^{T}Q\\cdot {Q}^{T}({P}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={P}^{T}(Q{Q}^{T})P \\\\  & ={P}^{T}(I)P \\\\  & ={P}^{T}P \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Computing the product $(PQ^T)\\cdot(PQ^T)^T ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(P{Q}^{T})\\cdot (P{Q}^{T})^{T} & =P{Q}^{T}\\cdot ({Q}^{T})^{T}{P}^{T} \\\\  & =P{Q}^{T}Q{P}^{T} \\\\  & =P({Q}^{T}Q){P}^{T} \\\\  & =P(I){P}^{T} \\\\  & =P{P}^{T} \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da20ffe7-173b-5fc0-8629-3b9b70222860" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134226 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98a3aa8d-63d4-5d00-8d79-78492a9bb759" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131396} {:answer/id #uuid "df9b02c6-0d93-58b0-9897-89434baadda3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131397} {:answer/id #uuid "438c5707-92f9-56c4-a1b6-7ea0a21908c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131398} {:answer/id #uuid "456662cd-2db3-5039-8959-d51ae15734c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131399} {:answer/id #uuid "6b2bf846-e3b5-5d17-ae90-92c41bbc11bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131397} :answer-field/id #uuid "67481058-b038-573b-864d-de6829a1fd78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131395}] :question/id #uuid "28eca231-06c6-5db5-8cec-335cddc2ad41" :question/math-academy-id "q-39618" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the $n\\times n$ orthogonal matrices $P$ and $Q?$\n\nI. $Q$ is invertible\n\nII. $PQ$ is always orthogonal\n\nIII. $P+Q$ is always orthogonal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The inverse of an orthogonal matrix $Q$ is given by ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the product $(PQ)\\cdot (PQ)^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(PQ)\\cdot (PQ)^{T} & =PQ\\cdot {Q}^{T}{P}^{T} \\\\  & =P(Q{Q}^{T}){P}^{T} \\\\  & =P(I){P}^{T} \\\\  & =P{P}^{T} \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the following sum of two orthogonal matrices:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe resulting matrix is not orthogonal since, for example,it is not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134475 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4b8b9d2-089a-5dba-b6ab-2370cafe71c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448690} {:answer/id #uuid "74213575-ddc8-5213-b2ce-a59e501dc1ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448691} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cebde04-2f8f-5849-8618-d6c2a4e0afbc" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449385} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49fafbe7-10fb-5c34-b26c-5b9891c6d338" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448692} {:answer/id #uuid "041b9065-8c9f-5ab2-b38c-d4d4aa44d1c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448693} :answer-field/id #uuid "4867348a-6919-5bb7-95d2-3a16f8877ca0" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449386} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a7cf8c7-d729-5add-8df3-4fe73a945535" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448694} {:answer/id #uuid "e6f0d748-cf94-5065-86a9-c7923f501c4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448695}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448694} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e31e2bd-4923-5578-a451-2148c2c56f5a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449387}] :question/id #uuid "14d8b1c2-9248-513d-b061-309a07f2a6a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-47458" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the orthogonal matrix $Q?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${Q}^{T}$ is orthogonal\n- **II.** The identity matrix $I$ is orthogonal\n- **III.** $2Q$ is orthogonal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the product ${Q}^{T}\\cdot ({Q}^{T})^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{T}\\cdot ({Q}^{T})^{T} & ={Q}^{T}Q \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The columns of the identity matrix are orthonormal. Therefore, $I$ is an orthogonal matrix.\n\n- Statement III is false. Computing the product $2Q\\cdot (2Q)^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2Q\\cdot (2Q)^{T} & =2Q\\cdot 2{Q}^{T} \\\\  & =4Q{Q}^{T} \\\\  & =4I \\\\  & \\ne I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272134 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98311a5d-c789-50b1-998c-82789cf3e15c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464132} {:answer/id #uuid "4a1caad2-1552-53b2-ad65-0faf937f9cf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464133} :answer-field/id #uuid "94d3e7a6-f070-518f-bca6-72c0a2e523f0" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465000} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2950a552-7b64-54f3-9df5-37fbc1daf708" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464134} {:answer/id #uuid "57bc0bcf-2d74-5b42-80b8-a9462aac80d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464134} :answer-field/id #uuid "11e24475-4728-5731-901b-4204e0ce6cef" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465001} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "427e5f77-75e3-5ea5-8f93-8f5a66733617" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464136} {:answer/id #uuid "01222f75-c4bd-542d-8467-b02dd4c8ea9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464136} :answer-field/id #uuid "d74724c4-d89e-52f6-b857-07eecbd822cb" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465002}] :question/id #uuid "a3d61daa-c943-5ecf-a98c-7f9bcd210491" :question/math-academy-id "q-140565" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the $n\\times n$ orthogonal matrix $Q$ and the $n\\times n$ matrix $P?$\n- **I.** If $\\lambda $ is a real eigenvalue of $Q,$ then $\\lambda =\\pm 1$\n- **II.** If the determinant of $P$ is either $1$ or $-1,$ then $P$ is orthogonal\n- **III.** $Q-I$ is singular\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $\\lambda $ is an eigenvalue of an orthogonal matrix $Q$ then $\\lambda =\\pm 1$\n\n- Statement II is false. Every orthogonal matrix has the determinant equal to $\\pm 1$, but not every matrix with the determinant equal to $\\pm 1$ is orthogonal. As a counterexample, consider the following matrix:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of $P$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P)=2\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 1=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n however, $P$ is not orthogonal since, for example, the first column is not normalized.\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the following orthogonal matrix:\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\nQ-I=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & -\\frac{2}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand $Q-I$ is not singular since, for example, it doesn't have proportional rows or columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354304 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb81d4ca-b624-5bd6-b5d6-8848a582c1a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451851} {:answer/id #uuid "072d8636-45df-522c-a21c-11c80be3c9aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451852} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ead3048-fa8d-5561-beeb-c20e86a46f07" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452773} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19f8ac0c-f828-5cbe-b1a1-9ac2fe52a581" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451853} {:answer/id #uuid "78608524-9f24-5352-8c0a-c522e7bb20f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451853} :answer-field/id #uuid "416ea6c4-aa3e-5622-ad1a-5f5c04bbc3f8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452774} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa1ef54f-6746-5e13-8812-1a43c4af52f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451855} {:answer/id #uuid "258461fb-779d-533f-b3c8-2a096b2dcdc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451856} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bed2a41-9fc5-5095-9e22-bb769fd4e778" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452775}] :question/id #uuid "f6582765-66f3-5651-9e94-833ca793221c" :question/math-academy-id "q-84347" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the orthogonal matrix $P?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${P}^{T}$ is orthogonal\n- **II.** ${P}^{-1}$ is **not** orthogonal\n- **III.** $-P$ is orthogonal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the product ${P}^{T}\\cdot ({P}^{T})^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{T}\\cdot ({P}^{T})^{T} & ={P}^{T}\\cdot P \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the product ${P}^{-1}\\cdot ({P}^{-1})^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1}\\cdot ({P}^{-1})^{T} & ={P}^{T}\\cdot ({P}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={P}^{T}P \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Computing the product $(-P)\\cdot (-P)^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-P)\\cdot (-P)^{T} & =(-P)\\cdot (-{P}^{T}) \\\\  & =P{P}^{T} \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354306 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "041bfe5b-590c-52a1-8abd-392d6a06b4fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451857} {:answer/id #uuid "bd73da35-a2bd-569b-aa95-e7ace5216014" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451857} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9fb4fbc-f699-56e7-8e73-ae2329de9b16" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452776} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6208240c-6e58-5465-95d8-6fd29d0111db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451859} {:answer/id #uuid "4e21d8f3-1ca2-507a-bffd-6e6526106a3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451860} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f64777b-1c75-5375-87b9-c74f54bdadeb" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452777} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a06b30fc-d6a0-5b6e-a013-87d9ef07f287" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451861} {:answer/id #uuid "01225e45-0e4a-533b-a1f7-48bca794d141" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451861} :answer-field/id #uuid "90d0e01e-ccc5-5919-850a-7e7c5a404273" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452778}] :question/id #uuid "55240a89-c0cc-59a9-b892-0f2b31965d95" :question/math-academy-id "q-84352" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the orthogonal matrix $Q?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $5Q$ is also orthogonal\n- **II.** If $A=QB{Q}^{T}$ then $|det(A)|=|det(B)|$\n- **III.** ${Q}^{3}$ is the zero matrix" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the product $5Q\\cdot 5{Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5Q\\cdot (5Q)^{T} & =5Q\\cdot 5{Q}^{T} \\\\  & =25Q{Q}^{T} \\\\  & =25I \\\\  & \\ne I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing $|det(A)|,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(A)| & =|det(QB{Q}^{T})| \\\\  & =|det(Q)|\\cdot |det(B)|\\cdot |det({Q}^{T})| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |det(B)|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|det(B)|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Let's consider the identity matrix\n\n$$\nQ=I=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe have that $Q$ is an orthogonal matrix and also ${Q}^{3}=Q\\ne O.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354339 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4e358b5-97fa-5dc0-9ec4-19466342da0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469410} {:answer/id #uuid "6b93d7f8-3a76-5294-8c4b-a64beffd4684" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469410} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a1473c4-f58e-5b7d-8f68-b11f5d273326" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470317} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "041c92fd-b654-5bac-8d90-25928f746218" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469412} {:answer/id #uuid "2d632d7e-d7b3-5411-87cd-979fb2168d8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469412} :answer-field/id #uuid "577e09cf-386e-5aa7-97ec-f5672711e8a1" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470318} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfb7808c-9349-508d-be48-6a448e81e286" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469414} {:answer/id #uuid "2e2826a9-8fd1-543d-8c80-03ed36d83923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469414} :answer-field/id #uuid "fefed51c-a546-5c98-b8b5-becce51ddb02" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470319}] :question/id #uuid "27c490dd-1808-531c-8cd0-cdd298d3c924" :question/math-academy-id "q-84786" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the orthogonal matrix $Q?$\n- **I.** $3Q$ is orthogonal\n- **II.** ${Q}^{T}$ is **not** orthogonal\n- **III.** ${Q}^{5}$ is the zero matrix\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the product $3Q\\cdot (3Q)^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3Q\\cdot (3Q)^{T} & =3Q\\cdot 3{Q}^{T} \\\\  & =9Q{Q}^{T} \\\\  & =9I \\\\  & \\ne I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the product ${Q}^{T}\\cdot ({Q}^{T})^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{T}\\cdot ({Q}^{T})^{T} & ={Q}^{T}Q \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Let's consider the identity matrix\n\n$$\nQ=I=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe have that $Q$ is an orthogonal matrix and also ${Q}^{5}=Q\\ne O.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366483 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b65968b-6028-551a-9631-58b67db16afa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469374} {:answer/id #uuid "a75e0f1b-240f-5f97-b88c-017b10166d2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469374} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7a2f7a7-5e01-577f-9f82-f523dd836563" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470299} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8af276a-607f-58cb-95a3-dfb795f49d12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469376} {:answer/id #uuid "de04d0f8-5260-5b89-a3cd-b666a82c85fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469376} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d01531c-c3a3-5572-8137-9d105c60ab89" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470300} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0feb765d-c901-5436-a1e5-1524e2608115" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469378} {:answer/id #uuid "aadde301-7372-5694-8cca-d9b6c7b72bd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469378} :answer-field/id #uuid "e169f11f-de5b-5f0d-ad57-d18c3a6c238d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470301}] :question/id #uuid "e6401598-f82b-58db-b408-23496dde6519" :question/math-academy-id "q-84344" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the $n\\times n$ orthogonal matrices $P$ and $Q?$\n- **I.** $2PQ$ is always orthogonal\n- **II.** $P-Q$ is always orthogonal\n- **III.** $P$ is **not** invertible\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the product $(2PQ)\\cdot (2PQ)^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2PQ)\\cdot (2PQ)^{T} & =2PQ\\cdot 2{Q}^{T}{P}^{T} \\\\  & =4P(Q{Q}^{T}){P}^{T} \\\\  & =4P(I){P}^{T} \\\\  & =4I \\\\  & \\ne I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the following subtraction of two orthogonal matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe resulting matrix is not orthogonal since, for example, its first column is not normalized.\n\n- Statement III is false. The inverse of an orthogonal matrix $P$ is given by ${P}^{-1}={P}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366490 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a454a558-e9c9-559c-8c56-52e733918130" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469380} {:answer/id #uuid "93599e5b-ce93-5b16-a5b8-85ac566f908e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469381} :answer-field/id #uuid "08b21958-5070-57d3-ab7f-c8d4fa998b69" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470302} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "861675fc-dffe-5e49-8d64-223921d9e84a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469382} {:answer/id #uuid "e927ecab-f475-5bf8-84b4-be36a9c65426" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469383} :answer-field/id #uuid "600fda14-a171-59e1-b8a6-d69e1c29474d" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470303} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d0a2b49-a427-56fc-9dbd-1ad4ffd42ff4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469384} {:answer/id #uuid "a9ef53d4-cbb1-5d21-b743-dd86c3da732c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469385} :answer-field/id #uuid "546258ed-c776-5904-bc1f-596ecebf3ec5" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470304}] :question/id #uuid "dcdd2917-752d-5fd1-ac2a-6a285cbe6a4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-84353" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the $n\\times n$ orthogonal matrices $P$ and $Q?$\n- **I.** $Q$ is invertible\n- **II.** ${P}^{T}$ is orthogonal\n- **III.** ${Q}^{-1}$ is always orthogonal\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The inverse of an orthogonal matrix $Q$ is given by ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the product ${P}^{T}\\cdot ({P}^{T})^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{T}\\cdot ({P}^{T})^{T} & ={P}^{T}\\cdot P \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Computing the product ${Q}^{-1}\\cdot ({Q}^{-1})^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{-1}\\cdot ({Q}^{-1})^{T} & ={Q}^{T}\\cdot ({Q}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={Q}^{T}Q \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485514 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c437605-0d0b-5911-8b6f-efde64e63238" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484907} {:answer/id #uuid "4b086b6e-3f56-53aa-ab01-3ec1ea51c4f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484982} {:answer/id #uuid "7328d2ac-ec4a-5f15-8f58-faebd2e52167" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485064} {:answer/id #uuid "7663b203-9ab4-5d32-b8ff-e2d87fc22238" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485070} {:answer/id #uuid "9697230b-8f6a-5224-a0f4-267b300f27ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484907} :answer-field/id #uuid "048ab02b-a33b-534b-a1c7-2bf319cf0b58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485366}] :question/id #uuid "35c37699-f2ab-5493-8b62-dd508d98be99" :question/math-academy-id "q-84784" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the $n\\times n$ orthogonal matrices $P$ and $Q?$\n\nI. If $A=PB{P}^{-1}$ then $|det(A)|=|det(B)|$\n\nII. $PQ$ is always orthogonal\n\nIII. $2P+Q$ is **not** always orthogonal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing $|det(A)|,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(A)| & =|det(PB{P}^{-1})| \\\\  & =|det(PB{P}^{T})| \\\\  & =|det(P)|\\cdot |det(B)|\\cdot |det({P}^{T})| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |det(B)|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|det(B)|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the product $(PQ)\\cdot (PQ)^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PQ\\cdot (PQ)^{T} & =(PQ)\\cdot {Q}^{T}{P}^{T} \\\\  & =P(Q{Q}^{T}){P}^{T} \\\\  & =P(I){P}^{T} \\\\  & =P{P}^{T} \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. As a counterexample, consider the following sum involving two orthogonal matrices:\n\n$$\n2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe resulting matrix is not orthogonal since, for example, the first column is not normalized.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485529 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5926b979-2296-5e06-b854-1498d765329c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485018} {:answer/id #uuid "887a1bd2-c587-5e95-a0cc-d565e244878d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485114} {:answer/id #uuid "9f356501-6df9-5979-9372-357a6a302ea9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485161} {:answer/id #uuid "b7b65862-95be-58c5-99d4-ab0f73d0d20a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485213} {:answer/id #uuid "d13939e6-36f9-5572-80e9-e753d41ea7a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485161} :answer-field/id #uuid "70e8c2d5-fce1-5f92-9639-37c01a107aaf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485412}] :question/id #uuid "4cd1f663-1b26-5239-80df-41dfbaf9dd53" :question/math-academy-id "q-140564" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the orthogonal matrix $Q?$\n\nI. ${Q}^{2}$ is orthogonal\n\nII. $Q$ is a diagonal matrix\n\nIII. $Q-I$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the product ${Q}^{2}\\cdot ({Q}^{2})^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{2}\\cdot ({Q}^{2})^{T} & =(Q\\cdot Q)\\cdot (Q\\cdot Q)^{T} \\\\  & =(Q\\cdot Q)\\cdot ({Q}^{T}\\cdot {Q}^{T}) \\\\  & =Q\\cdot (Q\\cdot {Q}^{T})\\cdot {Q}^{T} \\\\  & =Q(I){Q}^{T} \\\\  & =Q{Q}^{T} \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix:\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$Q$ is an orthogonal matrix, but it is not a diagonal matrix.\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, let $Q=I$ be the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix. Then, \n\n$$\nQ-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe $2\\times 2$  zero matrix, which is not an invertible matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521553 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "297ecca7-1d04-565f-97fa-4bf34f030962" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520853} {:answer/id #uuid "38cd3098-2a5e-55de-9315-3b8f18a09933" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520887} {:answer/id #uuid "98bf55fb-3a39-5303-9541-a60e5da17d4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521111} {:answer/id #uuid "a32d8fdf-be70-57b2-8de9-9bbc2f2e4992" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521131} {:answer/id #uuid "b2930480-2eb9-594e-9c28-dd13242c011e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521162} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9d10573-cd86-5734-a745-dadd21dc2271" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521444}] :question/id #uuid "d61adfc9-5b12-5408-9a76-aa7eede82be4" :question/math-academy-id "q-41715" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the orthogonal matrix $Q?$\n\nI. $Q{Q}^{T}=I$\n\nII. ${Q}^{2}$ is the zero matrix\n\nIII. If $A=QB{Q}^{-1}$ then $|det(A)|=|det(B)|$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true.\nIt's true by definition.\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider the identity matrix\n\n$$\nQ=I=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe have that $Q$ is an orthogonal matrix and also ${Q}^{2}=Q\\ne O.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Computing $|det(A)|,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(A)| & =|det(QB{Q}^{-1})| \\\\  & =|det(QB{Q}^{T})| \\\\  & =|det(Q)|\\cdot |det(B)|\\cdot |det({Q}^{T})| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |det(B)|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|det(B)|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Matrices"}] :topic/math-academy-id 2105 :topic/title "Orthogonal Matrices"}]}
